import sys

sys.dont_write_bytecode = True

USAGE = """\
usage: scripts/mod/compare [--project brotli|re2|brotli-tidy|brotli-tidy-fail]... [--case GLOB]... [--blaze BIN]
                           [--side oracle|blaze|both|none] [--list] [--show NAME]
                           [--json FILE]
       scripts/mod/compare selftest
       scripts/mod/compare help

Runs a fixed list of `mod` invocations (dump_repo_mapping, show_repo, graph, deps, all_paths,
path, explain, show_extension, tidy and their error cases) with the oracle
(~/.cache/blaze/oracle/bazel-9.3.2-linux-x86_64) and with Blaze (--blaze, default
target/debug/blaze) on work copies of the corpus projects (and of two fixtures, brotli-tidy and brotli-tidy-fail:
brotli plus a local module extension whose use_repo calls disagree with its
extension_metadata, defined in this script), and compares exit code, stdout and
stderr byte for byte. For `tidy` cases the resulting MODULE.bazel is compared too.

How each command runs (as scripts/oracle/record and scripts/w4a/run do): cwd is the work copy
~/.cache/blaze/work/mod-compare/<side>/<project> (rsync of ~/.cache/blaze/corpus/<project>
minus bazel-* and .git; refreshed at the start and before and after every tidy case); env -i HOME,
PATH=/usr/bin:/bin; startup --output_user_root=~/.cache/blaze/oracle-out/mod-compare-<side>
--nohome_rc --nosystem_rc; command options --repository_cache=~/.cache/blaze/repo-cache
--color=no --curses=no; under systemd-run --scope MemoryMax=12G MemorySwapMax=0. Before the
cases each side runs one discarded `mod graph` (fetches the module graph and the repositories
it needs into the output base). The oracle's server is shut down before every case, so each
case runs in a new server as every Blaze command does (a server that already evaluated a
module extension does not repeat its warnings), and at the end.

Comparison: the termination, the whole stdout, the whole stderr and (tidy) the resulting
MODULE.bazel, after the exclusion rules of scripts/lib/compare_common.py (VOLATILE_RULES;
`paths`: each side's output user root and work copy, also through symlinks; `output_base_hash`:
32-hex-digit directory names directly under the output user root; compare_common's
`no_actions_running` covers the UI's timer-driven " no actions running" lines and `blank` the
blank stderr lines,
among them the one UiEventHandler.completeBuild adds after stdout that did not end in a
newline), listed in the
report with the lines each changed or removed on each side. Both sides start each case on a
fresh server (the oracle's is shut down before every case), so the "Starting local Bazel
server" line is compared like any other.

Output: one line per case, the exclusions, the denominator; the captured files are in
~/.cache/blaze/work/mod-compare/results/<side>/<project>/<case>.{rc,out,err,module,term}.
--show NAME prints a unified diff of one case (project/case). --side none only re-compares
captured files (it judges nothing new and never exits 0).

Guards (scripts/lib/compare_common.py): every command runs under a supervisor in the scope that
records how it really ended (`exit N`, `signal NAME`, `oom-kill`, `timeout` after 3600 s), kept
in <case>.term; the comparison uses it (<case>.rc is for reading only; a capture without .term
never matches). The terminations of the warm-up `mod graph` and of `shutdown` are listed (an
abnormal one fails the run). The oracle must be the pinned one (UPSTREAM.json). Each oracle
capture has <case>.key, written after the capture's files and holding their digests with the key
(oracle sha256, the argv and environment, the digest of the corpus checkout, the project's
overlay, the project and the cases before it; the files are raw, so this tool's sha256 is not in
it); --side blaze and --side none refuse a stored oracle capture whose key or files differ. Each
Blaze capture has <case>.capture.json (the binary's sha256 and the files' digests); --side oracle
and --side none judge a stored Blaze capture only when --blaze names the binary that made it.
Classes: IDENTICAL (all of it equal after the rules; the oracle exited 0, or DECLARED_EXIT names
the case with that exit code), DIFFERENT, BOTH-FAILED (equal failures not declared),
NOT-PORTED, ORACLE-UNEXPECTED, SKIPPED. The output starts with the repository HEAD and dirty
flag and both binaries' paths and sha256, and ends with the denominator; --json writes the
report. Exit 0 only when at least one case was compared and every selected case is IDENTICAL; 1
otherwise (an empty selection and --side none included); 2 on a refusal.
selftest drives this command line with fake binaries (compare_common.selftest_tool).
"""

